# Frontend Performance

Performance is part of UX.

## First question

Do not optimize blindly.

Identify actual bottlenecks when possible.

## Prefer

- server rendering where appropriate
- static rendering where appropriate
- browser-native APIs
- CSS over JS for visual effects
- responsive images
- lazy loading when appropriate
- code splitting where useful
- stable layouts

## Avoid

- unnecessary client-side JavaScript
- large dependencies for small features
- unnecessary global state
- expensive work on every render
- unnecessary effects
- repeated network requests
- layout thrashing
- unnecessary re-renders

## Images

Optimize:

- dimensions
- format
- loading strategy
- responsive behavior

Avoid shipping huge images to small displays.

## Fonts

Avoid unnecessary font weights and families.

## Animation

Prefer compositor-friendly properties where appropriate.

Avoid expensive animations that cause layout recalculation.

## Data fetching

Avoid duplicate requests.

Follow existing caching and data-fetching architecture.

## Performance vs readability

Do not introduce complicated optimization patterns without evidence.

Simple code is preferred until performance requirements justify complexity.